<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Northern Dobruja</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a> is the region between the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, including the port of Constanța, which forms the south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. Ottoman for over four centuries, it was awarded to Romania by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> in 1878 in exchange for southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>, which went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-dobruja">Southern Dobruja</a>, taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 1913, was returned in 1940.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/northern-dobruja.jpg" alt="Northern Dobruja" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Krzysztof Dudzik-Górnicki (User:ToSter) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constanta,_view_from_mosque_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
